# ArchLucid assessment scope — headline V1.1 vs procurement realism

## Headline scores (must follow)

When producing **weighted readiness**, **solution quality**, **go-to-market**, or **trust/procurement** assessments for this repo:

1. **`(A)` V1.1 delivery / product readiness (headline)** — score **only** commitments that are **in scope for V1 GA and the named V1.1 release-window engineering items** documented in **`docs/library/V1_SCOPE.md`**, **`docs/library/V1_DEFERRED.md`**, and **`docs/go-to-market/TRUST_CENTER.md`**.

2. **`(B)` Enterprise procurement realism (informational)** — discuss buyer friction (SOC 2 CPA reports, rigid RFP wording, procurement timing) honestly, with **explicit links** to roadmap and trust posture. **`(B)` must use weight `0`** when recomputing or narrating **`(A)`**. Do **not** fold **`(B)`** into pillar weights or the **`(A)`** composite unless the user explicitly asks for a **second blended score**.

## SOC 2 — always mention, never headline-penalize

- **`(A)` must not be reduced** for absence of **SOC 2 Type I / II CPA attestation**, **issued SOC report**, or ISO 27001 certificate. Those items are **not** V1 or V1.1 headline gates per **`docs/library/V1_DEFERRED.md` §6c** and **`docs/library/V1_SCOPE.md` §3**.

- **Still cover SOC credibly**: cite **self-assessment**, **trust center honesty**, **`docs/security/SOC2_SELF_ASSESSMENT_2026.md`**, **`docs/go-to-market/SOC2_ROADMAP.md`**, CAIQ/SIG/DPA/process narratives — then place **SOC 2 CPA gap** under **`§ Enterprise procurement realism (informational)`** or equivalent, not under **`Defects against V1.1 headline readiness`**.

- **Explicitly prohibited:** lowering **`(A)` Compliance / Procurement / Trust pillar scores solely because CPA SOC 2 is not issued; treating SOC 2 as a “formal assurance gap against V1.1 contract” unless the narrative labels that sentence as **`(B)` procurement-only**.

## Design partner — omit from headline defects; never score on `(A)`

- **Not a V1 gate:** A **signed or active design partner** (commercial motion described in **`docs/go-to-market/PRICING_PHILOSOPHY.md`**) is a **V1.1** commercial item per **`docs/library/V1_DEFERRED.md` §6b** and **`docs/library/V1_SCOPE.md` §3**. V1 GA and pilot success do **not** require it.

- **`(A)` must not be reduced** for absence of a design partner — not in Marketability, Stickiness, Proof-of-ROI, trust pillars, or “readiness” composites.

- **Narrative:** Do **not** add recurring assessment sections (defect lists, open-question dumps, improvement gates) whose **primary** gap is “no design partner yet.” **Do not** imply the product is blocked pending a design partner. If the user **explicitly** asks for GTM or pipeline analysis, mention design-partner status **only** under **`(B)` market-motion / procurement realism** (zero weight on **`(A)`**), briefly.

- **Explicitly prohibited:** lowering **`(A)`** because the repo or org has not closed a design-partner contract; framing headline readiness as conditional on “finding” a design partner.

## Extension SDK, MCP, and third-party plugin marketplace — headline non-gates

- **Not V1 headline gates:** Per **`docs/library/V1_SCOPE.md`** (including the **speculative ecosystem** row and the **MCP** row) and **`docs/library/V1_DEFERRED.md` §6d**, the following are **out of `(A)`** unless the user explicitly asks for a separate ecosystem score:

  1. **No public extension / plugin SDK** — absence of a published, productized third-party extension SDK (or equivalent store-facing developer kit) for building ArchLucid plugins.

  2. **MCP not in V1** — absence of an inbound MCP server / MCP transport in the **V1** shipping boundary. **MCP is a named V1.1 engineering candidate**, not a pilot or V1 GA gate; narrate under roadmap / **`(B)`** if useful.

  3. **No third-party plugin marketplace** — absence of a customer-facing marketplace or agent store for third-party plugins (distinct from **Azure commercial marketplace** billing/GTM for ArchLucid’s own offer).

- **`(A)` must not be reduced** for any of the above in integrability, ecosystem, marketability, or “platform openness” pillars **unless `V1_SCOPE.md` / `V1_DEFERRED.md` explicitly lists the item as an in-window V1 or V1.1 engineering commitment you are scoring.**

- **Explicitly prohibited:** treating “no public extension SDK,” “no MCP yet,” or “no third-party plugin marketplace” as defects against **V1.1 headline readiness** in **`(A)`**; folding them into weighted deductions without labeling them **`(B)`** optional ecosystem realism.

## Assistive technology user testing — headline non-gate

- **Not a headline delivery gate for `(A)`:** Absence of **participant user testing with assistive technologies** (e.g. scheduled research sessions with screen-reader or other AT users as primary validation) is **out of scope** for headline readiness scoring. ArchLucid’s **projected primary users and buyers** are **not** modeled as requiring or valuing that class of study as a product commitment in **`(A)`**.

- **`(A)` must not be reduced** for “no AT user testing,” “no screen reader usability panel,” or similar — not under Trust, Quality, Inclusive design, or procurement-adjacent pillars unless **`V1_SCOPE.md` / `V1_DEFERRED.md` / `TRUST_CENTER.md`** explicitly names such testing as an in-window commitment.

- **Does not negate honest narrative:** If the assessment discusses **inclusive design**, **Section 508**, or **buyer accessibility questionnaires**, place material obligations, regulatory risk, or stretch goals under **`(B)` informational** (or a user-requested accessibility deep-dive), **without** treating missing AT user studies as a weighted defect inside **`(A)`**.

- **Explicitly prohibited:** lowering **`(A)`** solely because the org has not run user tests with assistive technologies; implying V1.1 headline readiness is deficient for lacking that research program.

## Hosted SaaS real LLM — not a sales-engineer gate for `(A)`

- **`(A)` must not be reduced** for lack of **sales-engineer-assisted onboarding** to obtain **real LLM** execution on **ArchLucid-hosted** SaaS. Per **`docs/library/V1_SCOPE.md` §2.4**, **platform-provisioned Azure OpenAI** on the standard hosted path is the **V1** posture; optional white-glove for **non-LLM** topics (identity, networking, procurement) belongs in **`(B)`** narrative when relevant — not as a headline defect that **real** LLM is “blocked” pending SE setup.

- **Explicitly prohibited:** claiming **Customer self-sufficiency**, **Time-to-Value**, or **Adoption friction** headline gaps **solely** because buyers were not walked through **LLM credential** setup on hosted SaaS — that contradicts the **V1** contract unless **`V1_SCOPE.md`** is superseded by an explicit owner decision.

## Alignment with broader realism rules

Workspace **`Enterprise-Realism.mdc`** still applies for design trade-offs. For **numerical readiness scoring**, this rule **wins**: organizational procurement pressure is modeled in **`(B)`**, not as hidden deductions inside **`(A)`**.

